Friedman was introduced to the Association by her friend and [[Beach Boys]] lyricist [[Van Dyke Parks]]. She wrote "Windy" in [[waltz]] [[Time signature|time]], but the group's producer [[Bones Howe]] changed it to the more common '''[[Time signature|{{Music|time|4|4|size=}}]]''' to ensure the song's commercial appeal.<ref name="songfacts" />{{Efn|Similar to "[[Cherish (The Association song)|Cherish]]", in that its producer [[Curt Boettcher]] sped the tempo of the song to be played on AM radio}}

Friedman had written the song about a man but the Association changed the gender in the lyrics.<ref>{{cite interview |url=http://popcultureaddict.com/interviews/larryramos/ |title=Along Comes Larry: A Conversation with Larry Ramos |subject=Ramos, Larry |interviewer=Sam Tweedle |date=August 2011 |website=Confessions of a Pop Culture Addict |access-date=12 December 2016 |quote=Well, the song is not about a girl. It's about a guy. It was written by a gal named Ruthann Friedman, and she wrote it about this guy named Windy. He was a San Francisco/Haight-Ashbury type and if you listen to it with that in mind you can see how it's a totally different type of song than if you think of it being about a girl. It's a happy tune, but if you listen to the lyrics you can see how it's about a guy stoned out of his mind. (Laughs) ''Windy has stormy eyes / that flash at the sound of lies / and Windy has wings to fly / Up above the clouds.'' I mean the guy's completely gone! Anyways, the guy died from an O.D. It's sad, but then that's what happens.}}</ref><ref name="songfacts" /><ref>{{Cite web|date=2018-10-15|title=The Number Ones: The Association's "Windy"|url=https://www.stereogum.com/2018612/the-number-ones-the-associations-windy/columns/the-number-ones/|access-date=2021-03-23|website=Stereogum|language=en}}</ref> In an interview with [[Songfacts]], she said:<ref name="songfacts" /><ref>{{Cite web|date=2015-05-09|title=Who, Exactly, Was the Song "Windy" About?|url=http://legendsrevealed.com/entertainment/2015/05/08/who-exactly-was-the-song-windy-about/|access-date=2021-03-23|website=Entertainment Legends Revealed!}}</ref>{{quote|I have heard so many different permutations of what the song was about. Here is the TRUTH. I was sitting on my bed – the apartment on the first floor of [[David Crosby]]’s house in Beverly Glenn [sic] – and there was a fellow who came to visit and was sitting there staring at me as if he was going to suck the life out of me. So I started to fantasize about what kind of a guy I would like to be with, and that was Windy – a guy (fantasy).}}

However, in another interview with Songfacts in 2014, she explained that the song was about herself:<ref>{{Cite web|last=Songfacts|title=Ruthann Friedman - "Windy" : They're Playing My Song|url=https://www.songfacts.com/blog/playingmysong/ruthann-friedman-windy|access-date=2021-03-23|website=www.songfacts.com|language=en}}</ref>

{{quote|These days, looking back at myself in my mid to late 20s, I finally realized I was talking about me in that song, and how I wanted to be.}}

A demo of the song, recorded by Ruthann Friedman appears on her album, ''[[Windy: A Ruthann Friedman Songbook]]''.<ref>''The Second Disc'', June 25, 2013 - [https://theseconddisc.com/2013/06/25/windy-a-ruthann-friedman-songbook-explores-the-solo-side-of-windy-songwriter/ NEWS, "Windy: A Ruthann Friedman Songbook" Explores The Solo Side of The Songwriter By Joe Marchese]</ref>

